<p class="sst-lead">A ridge hides in plain sight. Project the data onto a guessed direction \(u\) and look at the
<strong>sufficient summary plot</strong>, \(f\) vs \(u^{\top}x\).</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>Rotate \(u\). Off the active direction, the points are a cloud &mdash; the same \(u^{\top}x\) maps to many
  \(f\) values. On it, they <strong>collapse to a single curve</strong> \(g\): the function really is 1-D.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">That collapse is dimension reduction made visible. (The spread bottoms out at a small nonzero
  value here &mdash; an artifact of binning the residual, left honest rather than rounded to zero.)</p>
</div>
